// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
/*
 * Realtek Luna (RTL960x) -- what every part of the family shares.
 *
 * ★★★ THE KERNEL COMMAND LINE IS FAMILY CONFIGURATION, NOT A BOARD FACT, AND
 * IT MUST NOT BE INHERITED FROM THE BOOTLOADER (measured 2026-08-27 on the
 * X111W).  `printenv bootargs` at that board's U-Boot prompt answers with an
 * EMPTY value -- the vendor bootloader passes the kernel NOTHING -- so
 * everything the kernel gets has to come from here.  `uboot.py`'s own comment
 * assumed the opposite ("the existing line carries console=, mtdparts= and
 * root="); that assumption is refuted for this family.
 *
 * ★★ WHAT IT COST: the two boards in this tree disagreed, and only one of them
 * could be debugged.
 *
 *     rtl9602c_x111w.dts     bootargs = "console=ttyS0,115200"
 *     rtl9607c_engboard.dts  bootargs = "console=ttyS0,115200 earlycon
 *                                        ignore_loglevel keep_bootcon"
 *
 * With the first line the X111W's kernel prints NOTHING between "Starting
 * kernel ..." and OpenWrt's userspace preinit: no version banner, no memory
 * map, not one driver probe.  The UART works throughout -- the arch code's own
 * putchar markers come out and userspace reaches /dev/console -- so the board
 * looks alive and says nothing, and every conclusion drawn about its ~29 s
 * reset was reached WITHOUT A KERNEL LOG.  The moment `ignore_loglevel` was
 * added by hand the cause was visible in one boot.
 *
 * ⇒ so the line lives ONCE, here, and the whole family gets a kernel that can
 *   be read.  A board that needs something else adds it to its own `chosen`;
 *   the properties merge by node path.
 *
 * ★ WHY THESE FOUR:
 *   console=ttyS0,115200  the family's UART: serial@18002000 at the same
 *                         address and the same rate on every Luna part here.
 *   earlycon              printk before the 8250 driver binds -- without it a
 *                         fault in early init is silent by construction.
 *   keep_bootcon          do NOT drop the early console when the real one
 *                         registers; the hand-over window is exactly where an
 *                         early driver failure would otherwise vanish.
 *   ignore_loglevel       the default level hides driver messages, which is
 *                         what made this board undebuggable.
 *
 * ⚠ THIS IS A DEVELOPMENT DEFAULT AND IT IS DELIBERATE.  These units are being
 *   brought up and certified; a silent kernel costs days, and the cost of the
 *   verbosity is console bytes.  When a board ships, drop `ignore_loglevel`
 *   from its own `chosen` -- and say so there, so the next reader knows the
 *   silence was chosen rather than inherited.
 */

/ {
	chosen {
		bootargs = "console=ttyS0,115200 earlycon keep_bootcon ignore_loglevel";
	};
};
